{#- Navigation layout variables (docs/features/navigation README §4.2, §4.3, §5; implementation plan F-03, decisions
    17, 18). Included at the end of base.html.twig's critical <style>: one rule per line, plain CSS, no Twig output.
    The only place these lengths are set - JavaScript never writes them (NavLayoutCssTest); the browser decides from
    media queries and <html> attributes before first paint. SCSS reads them through assets/styles/_nav-tokens.scss.

    --header-height  static height of the top bar the sticky/fixed sub-bars sit under (their layout anchor): 52 px on
                     phones, 64 px on desktop (NAV-03), 0 on phones under 480 px tall - the bar scrolls with the page
                     there (no sticky sub-bar would sit under it).
    --topbar-h       live height of the top bar (0 while hidden): scroll margins.
    --topbar-shift   how far the hidden top bar moved up: sub-bars follow it with `translate` (mixin nav-follow-topbar).
    --bottom-nav-height  phone bottom bar + safe area, 0 while no visible .bottom-nav, with a running stopwatch and in
                     the app. Every fixed bottom element sits above it (mixin nav-bottom).
    --nav-left       width of the desktop Menu column / rail (NAV-13, README §4.3 / D16): 0 on phones and 768-1023 px;
                     88 px (the rail) from 1024 px; 256 px (the docked column) at 1280-1439 px with
                     html[data-nav-layout="docked"] and from 1440 px unless html[data-nav-layout="rail"] - the attribute
                     is the player's choice per device, set before paint by assets/head/nav_layout.js. 1440 px is the one
                     switch of D16's fold rule ($nav-dock-default-from in _nav-layout.scss). <body> overrides it: a page
                     without the bars (body.no-nav-chrome: bar-free templates, the app) keeps 0, the `menu` page
                     (body.no-nav-column: the Menu is its content) keeps the rail; print 0. main, the footer and the old
                     header rows (until NAV-06) start right of it - the footer sits right of the column. hide_bottom_bar
                     hides the phone tab bar only: the rail stays on desktop (NAV-08). The old header
                     rows (.legacy-chrome, until NAV-06) are clipped at the content's right edge: in de/es/fr their tools
                     no longer fit next to the column and would scroll the page sideways.
    Under html[data-native-app] every reserved chrome size is 0 (plan §3.5 item 7).
    .menu-slot       the docked-column slot of the page's one #site-menu (MENU-01): hidden wherever there is no docked
                     column, shown where there is one - the column itself is #site-menu in it, fixed under the top bar,
                     256 px, its own scroller. Its groups folded by the player (html[data-nav-collapsed], disclosure
                     buttons) are hidden before paint; the place row heading a group stays.
                     The rail (NAV-13, R9) is .bottom-nav itself from 1024 px wherever the column is not docked: 88 px,
                     fixed under the top bar - one under it on the z-scale (1029: the bar's search panel and menus
                     overlap the column).
    .bottom-nav      the phone bottom bar's box (NAV-01, README §4.2): fixed, 56 px + the safe area (Chrome 135+ fast
                     path: bottom = inset - max inset, padding = max inset), none on desktop; <body> keeps its height
                     free at the end of the page. Its look and the label steps live in _nav-bars.scss.
    .nav-sprite      the icon sprite (_icons.html.twig) never takes room.
    .site-header     the top bar (NAV-03, README §4.1): sticky (decision 20), 52 px phones / 64 px desktop, static on
                     phones under 480 px tall; its tools 44 × 44 and 4 px apart; the search field fills the room.
                     Variants decided from the markup, before the stylesheet: a running stopwatch's pill takes the
                     field's place (search becomes the icon); the installed app's back button (.standalone-only, shown
                     under html.is-standalone) turns the field into the icon below 390 px, and leaves no search icon next
                     to a pill below 393 px. html[data-search-open] = the open search (global_search_controller.js):
                     the field fills the bar next to the logo and ×, the other tools step aside, the panel and the scrim
                     show. Closed, the panel, × and the scrim are display: none. The rest of its look: _nav-topbar.scss.
                     Desktop (NAV-04, README §4.3 / D16), one 64 px row that never hides: the phone ☰ gives way to the
                     desktop ☰ (first), + Add and the avatar ▾ (NAV-05) / guests' Sign in appear, the tools sit 8 px
                     apart. 768-1023 px: ☰ with its "Menu" label, the logo mark, the places Home · Puzzles · Community
                     (_places compact: icon over label, as the tab bar) and the 🔍 instead of the field - opened, the
                     field fills the bar next to the logo and ×; next to a running stopwatch's pill + Add is its icon
                     (its label stays the accessible name) and the tools sit 4 px apart. From 1024 px: the logo's
                     name, the field (up to 560 px), Feedback's icon instead of ?. From 1200 px: Feedback's and
                     Inbox's labels. + Add and Sign in: 44 px targets, drawn 40 px.
    Browsers without :has() reserve the bottom bar's height on phones anyway (no visible-bar test there).
    .site-header--auth  the sign-in / sign-up pages' header (NAV-08, _auth_header.html.twig): the top bar's box with the
                     logo and the language switch only - the switch at the right edge, its summary a 44 px target
                     without the disclosure marker before the stylesheet arrives.
    .bottom-nav--tabbar-hidden  a page that sets hide_bottom_bar (NAV-08, decision 46 - the multiscan scanner's action bar
                     takes the bottom edge): no tab bar on phones and no room kept for it; desktop keeps the places (the
                     rail, NAV-13). -#}
